- op: discuss  # e1eb
  at: '2026-11-11T03:26:38Z'  # e1eb
  by: agent  # e1eb
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e1eb
  clock: 4  # e1eb
  nonce: e1eb  # e1eb
  message: "Done in PR #2131. SHIPPED: (1) scripts/pytest_plugins/pytest_fail_grace.py, --fail-grace=N stops a run N seconds after the first failed report, with and without xdist, exit 1; (2) per-PR CI pytest step uses --fail-grace=15 (cron suites untouched); (3) smart-test targeted runs use it by default (SMART_TEST_FAIL_GRACE=0 or --fail-grace=0 runs everything; not on --full or explicitly named files); (4) smart-test hands pytest the run set failure-first (changed tests, coverage-selected, citation tests, rest) -- reorders, never removes; (5) smart-test-bed prints the first-MUST rank. BEHAVIOURAL EVIDENCE: throwaway project, 4 workers, grace 1 s: failing file first -> 3.7 s, last -> 11.4 s. The branch own run while five tests were red stopped at 92 s (1,989 tests) where the green run of that selection takes 295 s (4,426). SIZING MEASURED: PR pytest steps are bimodal, 15-46 s up to ~150 files, 11-28 min for 200-760 files; about 1 PR run in 9 ends red (120 ci_failed vs 973 merged). BED: first MUST never later, 4th -> 1st in 2 of 18 edits; run sets of 1-12 files, so this shows no harm and does not size the gain. NOT MEASURED, and said so in the commit: the spread of failures in real red CI runs (failing commits are superseded, logs not recoverable), so 15 s is the owner estimate; and xdist ordering on the real suite beyond the one observed red run. NOT DONE: failure-first ordering in CI (the manifest order is unchanged there; only the stop rule applies)."  # e1eb
